WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.767
When we are machine knitting,
the whole purpose of the yarn mass is

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.280
to keep steady,
even tension on your working yarn.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.600
But things can go wrong.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.280
If you hit a knot or the yarn gets caught
up somehow,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.160
maybe it gets caught up under the cone.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.160
You might have a few stitches
that are misformed and way too tight.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.720
The only remedy for this is to rip
out to this row and start again.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.647
Sometimes after you've ripped or you've
done some hand manipulation or changed

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:42.247
colors or for whatever reason,
the mast isn't engaged in putting

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.480
the proper tension on your working yarn.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.560
My first knitting teacher
taught me to milk the cow.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.320
Hold down on the yarn behind the tension
mast and make sure that the mast engages,

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.840
and it will ensure perfectly
formed stitches every time.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.840
It's a good habit to check.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.047
Any time you start knitting,
you first sit down to knit or you've

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.240
gotten up to take a break,
make sure that tension mast is engaged

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.680
and you've got nicely formed stitches.

